\noindent{\bf Introduction} 

Abelian varieties are some of the most studied higher dimensional algebraic
varieties. They appear to be very simple, as they are constructed by 
quotienting ${\Bbb C}^n$ by an integral lattice. However, there are many open 
problems concerning their geometry. One of the techniques that has been used to
investigate the properties of PPAVs is the study of the singularities
of their theta divisor. This connection was first 
exploited by Andreotti and Mayer [AM], in their study of the Schottky problem.
In another direction Kempf [Ke] showed that if $A$ is the Jacobian variety of some curve
then the theta divisor has only rational singularities.  
In a joint article [AD], Albarello and De Concini suggest that if $(A,\Theta )$
is an irreducible principally polarized abelian variety, then the singular
locus of the theta divisor has codimension at least 3 in $A$.
At any rate, Koll\'{a}r [K] proved that the singularities of the theta divisor
 on a principally polarized abelian variety are mild in the following sense:

\noindent
\proclaim{\rm {THEOREM (Koll\'{a}r)}}  Let $(A,\Theta )$ be a PPAV, then
the pair $(A,\Theta )$ is log canonical.
\endproclaim

This implies for example that if
$$\Sigma _k(\Theta )=\{ x\in A\ |\ mult_x(\Theta )\ge k\ \},$$
then every component of $\Sigma _k(\Theta )$
has codimension at least $k$ in $A$.

It is easy to construct examples in which the codimension is exactly
$k$, by just considering a $k$-fold product of PPAVs. Ein and Lazarsfeld
[EL] proved that this is essentially the only case in which
such a phenomenon can occur.
\proclaim {\rm {THEOREM (Ein, Lazarsfeld)}} If $\Theta \subset A$ is an 
irreducible theta divisor, then $\Theta$ is normal and has only
rational singularities.
\endproclaim 
Consequently, if $(A,\Theta )$ is any PPAV for which $\Sigma _k(\Theta )$ 
contains an irreducible
component of 
codimension $k$ then $(A,\Theta )$ splits as
a $k$-fold product of PPAVs.
They also generalized the result of Koll\'{a}r to
${\Bbb {Q}}$-divisors in the following sense:
\proclaim {\rm {THEOREM (Ein, Lazarsfeld)}} If $D$ is a divisor in the
linear series $|m\Theta |$, then the pair $(A,\frac {1}{m} D)$
is log-canonical.
\endproclaim
In the same spirit as the first of the two theorems of Ein and Lazarsfeld, 
we prove the following:
\proclaim{\rm {THEOREM 1}} Let 
$(A,\Theta )$ be a principally polarized abelian variety
and for $m\geq 1$ let $D$ be a divisor in the linear series
$| m\Theta |$ such that $\lfloor \frac {1}{m}D  \rfloor = 0$. Then
the pair $(A,\frac {1}{m}D)$ is log terminal.
\endproclaim
The condition that $\lfloor \frac {1}{m}D \rfloor =0$ is equivalent to
requiring that  the multiplicity of every irreducible component of $D$
is strictly less than $m$. 
\proclaim{\rm {COROLLARY 2}} Let $(A,\Theta )$ be a principally polarized 
abelian variety,
and for $m\geq 1$ let $D$ be a divisor in the linear series
$| m\Theta |$. If $k\geq 1$ is the greatest integer such that
$\Sigma _{mk}(D)$
contains an irreducible component
of codimension $k$, then $(A,\Theta )$ splits as a 
product of at least $k$ PPAVs   
$$(A,\Theta )\cong (A_1,\Theta _1)\times ... \times (A_k,\Theta _k) 
\times (A',\Theta ')$$
(the component $(A',\Theta ')$ being possibly trivial).
Moreover, the divisor $D$ decomposes as follows
$$D=m\sum _{i=1} ^k p_i^* (\Theta _i) + {p'} ^* D'$$
where $D' \in |m \Theta '|$, and the pair $(A',\frac {1}{m}D' )$ is log terminal.
\endproclaim
\noindent{{REMARK.}} The component $(A',\Theta ')$ is trivial precisely
when $D'=0$, ie when $\lfloor \frac {1}{m}D \rfloor =\frac {1}{m}D$.
Similarly $(A,\Theta )\cong (A',\Theta ')$ (ie $k=0$) precisely when
$\lfloor \frac {1}{m}D \rfloor =0$. This is the case treated in theorem 1.

