\noindent{\bf 2. Proofs} 
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Proof of theorem 1. Let $D$ be a divisor in the linear series 
$|m\Theta |$. Consider its decomposition into 
irreducible reduced components
$$D=\sum d_i D_i\ .$$
We may assume that $1\leqq d_i \leqq m-1$. Now consider a log resolution 
$f:X\longrightarrow A$ of the pair $(A, D)$. Define the sheaf
$$\L1 := \OO _X(f^{*} (\Theta )-\lfloor \frac {1}{m} f^{*} D\rfloor )$$
and observe that (cf. \cite {E})
$$f_* (\omega _X\otimes \L1 )=f_{*} (\omega _{X/A} \otimes f^{*} (\Theta )
-\lfloor \frac {1}{m} f^* D\rfloor )=\II (\frac {1}{m} D)\otimes \OO _A(
\Theta )$$
$$R^if_*(\omega _X\otimes \L1 )=0\ \ \forall \ i>0\ .$$
We must show that the multiplier ideal $\II :=\II (\frac {1}{m} D )$ is trivial.
As before, let
$$V_i:= \{ \PP \in Pic^0 (X) \ |\ h^i(\omega _X\otimes \L1 \otimes \PP )\neq 0\ \}$$ 
$$\ \ \ \ = \{ \PP \in Pic^0 (A) \ |\ h^i(\OO _A(\Theta )\otimes \II (\frac {1}{m} D)\otimes \PP ) \neq 0\ \},$$ 
and consider $S$ an irreducible component of $V_0$ of dimension $k$.

By Theorem 1.1.a, we have that $S$ is (a translate of) a subtorous of $Pic^0 (X)$,
so we may consider the dual abelian variety $C:=S^*$ and the dual map
$$\pi : A \longrightarrow C\ .$$
The component $S$ is not empty, as otherwise, we would have (Theorem 1.1.c)
$H^i(X,\omega _X\otimes \L1 \otimes \PP )=0$ for all ${\Cal {P}}\in Pic^0(X)$ 
and all integers $0\leqq i\leqq n$.
Therefore, by a result of Mukai [M], $\omega _X\otimes \L1 \otimes \PP =0$ 
and this is a contradiction.
\proclaim{\rm {PROPOSITION 2.1}} Let $F$ be any component of the divisor $D$ 
such that $\pi (F)=C$. 
Then 
$$F\subset Bs|\Theta \otimes \PP\otimes \II (\frac {1}{m} D)|$$
for general $\PP \in S$. 
\endproclaim

Proof.  Let $B:=f^* D=\sum b_i B_i$, then the divisor  
$\BB=\sum_{m \nmid b_i}B_i$
contains the proper transforms of all the components of $D$.
Now consider 
$$ H^0(X,\Omega ^{n-1}_X<\BB >\otimes \L1 \otimes \PP (-\BB ))
\overset {\wedge \omega } \to {\longrightarrow }
H^0(X,\omega _X\otimes \L1 \otimes \PP)\ \ \ \ \ \ (1)$$
where $\omega \in H^0(X,\Omega ^1_X)$ is a global holomorphic 1-form. 
By [T], this is the conjugate of the last map in the complex in theorem 1.1,d .
The conjugate $v=\overline {\omega }\in H^1(X,\OO _X)$ may be identified 
with a vector in the tangent space $T_{\PP }(Pic^0 (X))$. Recall that if
$v \notin T_{\PP }S$, then $(1)$ is onto. In order to recover the required 
information from (1), we must make the appropriate choice of the holomorphic
1-form $\omega$. To this end, consider any component $F$ of $D$ such that $\pi (F)=C$. Let $E$ be the proper transform of $F$ under the map $f:X\lra A$.
$E$ is a component of $\BB$. Let 
$p$ a general smooth point of the 
divisor $E$, and choose local parameters $z_1,...,z_n$ on an appropriate open set $U$,
such that $E$ is defined on $U$ 
by the equation $z_1=0$. Choose $\omega \in H^0(X,\Omega ^1_X)$ 
such that $\omega (p)=dz_1$. By choosing $p\in E$ generically, we may 
assume that $\overline {\omega }$ is not tangent to $S$, and so $(1)$ is onto.
Let $s$ be a section of  $H^0(X,\Omega ^{n-1}_X<\BB >
\otimes \L1 \otimes \PP (-\BB ))$. Then restricting to the open set $U$,
we may write
$$s=\sum \eta _i z_1 a_i\ ,$$
where $\eta _1= dz_2\wedge ... \wedge dz_n$,
$\eta _i= \frac {dz_1}{z_1}\wedge ...\wedge dz_{i-1} \wedge dz_{i+1}\wedge ...
  \wedge dz_n$ for $2\leqq i \leqq n$, and 
$a_i\in \Gamma (U,\L1 \otimes \PP )$. Therefore,
$$ (s\wedge \omega)_{(p)}=(a_1 z_1\ dz_1\wedge ...\wedge dz_n ) _{(p)}=0\ ,$$
so $p\in Bs |\omega _X\otimes \L1 \otimes \PP |$. Moreover,  
$p$ is a general point of 
$E$, so $E\in Bs|\omega _X\otimes \L1 \otimes \PP |$.
Since $$H^0(X,\omega _X \otimes \L1 \otimes \PP )\cong H^0(A,\OO _A(\Theta )
\otimes \PP \otimes \II )\ ,$$ and $E$ is the proper transform of $F$,
it follows that
$$ F\subset Bs|\Theta \otimes \PP\otimes \II (\frac {1}{m} D)|\ \ \square$$
   
\proclaim{\rm {COROLLARY 2.2}} The divisor
$$\Theta - \sum _{\pi (D_i)=C} D_i $$
is algebraically equivalent to an effective divisor, and hence
is nef. 
\endproclaim

Now consider the divisor
$$H:=D-\sum _{\pi (D_i)=C}d_iD_i\ .$$ 
Using the corollary, we may rewrite the divisor $H$ as a sum of an ample divisor, a nef divisor and effective divisor  
$$H\equiv \Theta +(m-1)(\Theta - \sum _{\pi (D_i)=C}D_i)+
\sum _{\pi (D_i)=C} (m-1-d_i)D_i\ .$$
On an PPAV, any effective divisor is nef, and the sum of
an ample divisor with a nef divisor is always ample.
Therefore the divisor 
$H$ is ample.

The theorem follows, since as $H$ is ample and contained in the 
pullback of a divisor on $C$, the map $\pi $ must be an isomorphism, and 
hence $S=Pic^0(A)$. So 
$$H^0(A, \OO _A(\Theta )\otimes \PP \otimes \II ( \frac {1} {m} D ) )\neq 0$$
 for all $\PP
\in Pic^0(A)$. This means that all translates of $\Theta $ must vanish
along the cosupport of $\II $, but then $\II =\OO _A\ \ \ \square $ 

Proof of corollary 2. In order 
to prove corollary 2, we will need the following lemmas:
\proclaim {\rm {LEMMA 2.2}} The components of the divisor $D\in |m\Theta |$
have multiplicity at most equal to $m$.
\endproclaim

Proof. Suppose that there exists a component $F$ of $D$ of multiplicity
$f>m$, and let 
$$D=fF+\sum d_iD_i$$
be a decomposition of $D$ into distinct irreducible components. 
We have the following numerical equivalence of $\QQ $-divisors 
$$\Theta -F\equiv _{\QQ } \frac {1}{m} (D - mF)\ .$$
Since $f\geq m+1$, it follows (after an easy calculation)
that $\frac {f-m}{m}\geq \frac {1}{m+1}\frac
{f}{m}$. 
Consequently 
$$(\Theta -F).C=\frac {1}{m} (D - mF).C
= \frac {1}{m}((f-m)F+\sum d_iD_i).C\geq$$
$$\geq  \frac {1}{m}(\frac {f}{m+1}F+\sum \frac {d_i}{m+1}D_i).C=(\frac {1}{m+1}) \Theta . C$$ 
for any curve $C$, and hence
$(\Theta -F)$ is ample. By Riemann-Roch, $h^0 (\Theta -F)>0$, 
so $h^0 (\Theta -F)=
h^0 (\Theta)=1$. This means that $(\Theta -F)^n=(\Theta )^n$. On an 
abelian variety this is a contradiction unless the component $F$ is empty 
$\square$

Now we may assume that the divisor $D$ decomposes as follows
$$D=\sum _{i=1}^{\delta } d_iD_i +m\Delta \ ,$$
where $1\leqq d_i \leqq m-1$, and $\Delta =\sum _{d_i=m}D_i$ is the reduced
Cartier divisor whose components are the components of $D$ of multiplicity 
exactly $m$.
\proclaim {\rm {LEMMA 2.3}} There exist PPAVs $(A_0,\Theta _0)$ and $(A',\Theta ')$,
and projections $p_0 :A\longrightarrow A_0$, 
$p' :A\longrightarrow A'$ such that the map 
$$(p_0\times p'):A\longrightarrow A_0\times A'$$
is an isomorphism of PPAVs. Moreover 
$$\Delta = p_0^* \Delta _0$$
$$D_i= {p'} ^* D_i'$$
where $\Delta _0 = \Theta _0$ and $D'= \sum _{i=1}^{\delta } d_i
D_i'\equiv m\Theta '$.
\endproclaim

Proof. We will use the notation and the results of [LB]
chapters 3.3 and 4.3. The divisor $m(\Theta -\Delta )$ is linearly equivalent
to the effective divisor $\sum d_iD_i$, hence
$\OO _A (m(\Theta -\Delta ))$ and $\OO _A (\Theta -\Delta )$
are positive semidefinite line bundles. Therefore, there
exists a topologically trivial line bundle $P\in Pic^0(A)$ such that
$h^0(\OO _A(\Theta -\Delta )\otimes P)\neq 0$. 
Define $p_0:A\longrightarrow A_0:=
A/K(\Delta )_0$ and 
$p':A\longrightarrow A':=
A/K(\Theta -\Delta +P)_0$.
By [LB] 3.3.2, there exist positive definite line bundles
${\Cal {M}}'$ on $A'$ and ${\Cal {M}}_0$ on
$A_0$ such that 
$${\Cal {O}} _A(\Delta )=p_0^*
{\Cal {M}}_0\ \ \ and \ \ \ H^0(A,{\Cal {O}} _A(\Delta ))
= p_0^*(H^0(A_0,{\Cal {M }}_0))$$
$${\Cal {O}} _A(\Theta -\Delta )\otimes P={p'}^*
{\Cal {M}}'\ \ \ and \ \ \ H^0(A,{\Cal {O}} _A(\Theta -\Delta  )\otimes P)
= {p'}^*(H^0(A',{\Cal {M }}'))$$
for some $P\in Pic^0(A)$. By the decomposition theorem
[LB] 4.3.1, the map
$$(p_0,p'):A\longrightarrow A_0\times A'$$
is an isomorphism of the PPAVs $(A,\Theta \otimes P)$ and $(A_0\times A',
{p_0}^*{\Cal {M}}_0\otimes {p'}^*{\Cal {M}}')$.
Finally, since $K(\Theta -\Delta )_0=K(
(\Theta -\Delta )\otimes P)_0=K(m(\Theta -\Delta ))_0$, it follows that
$$H^0(A,{\Cal {O}} _A(m(\Theta -\Delta ) ))
= {p'}^*(H^0(A',m{\Cal {M }}')),$$
so $\Delta =p_0^*\Delta _0$ and $\sum d _i
D_i = {p'}^* D'\ \ \ \ \square $

Suppose that there exists $S$ an irreducible component of 
$\Sigma _{mk} (D)$ of codimension $k$.
Then there exist positive integers $k_0$ and $k'$ such that
$mk=k_0+k'$ and 
$$S\subset \Sigma _{k_0} (m \Delta _0)\times \Sigma _{k'} 
(\sum _{i=1}^{\delta } d_iD_i')\ .$$

From the second of the two theorems due to Ein and Lazarsfeld,
we know that 
$$codim _{A_0} \Sigma _{k_0} (m \Delta _0)=codim _{A_0} 
\Sigma _{\lceil \frac {k_0}{m} \rceil } 
( \Delta _0) \geq \lceil \frac {k_0}{m}\rceil \ .$$
Also, by theorem 1, we know that if $k'>0$, then
$$codim _{A'} \Sigma _{k'}(\sum _{i=1}^{\delta } d_iD_i ')
\geq \lfloor \frac {k'}{m}\rfloor +1\ .$$
Therefore, 
$$k=codim _A S\geq codim _{A_0} \Sigma _{k_0} (m \Delta _0)+
codim _{A'} \Sigma _{k'}(\sum _{i=1}^{\delta } d_iD_i ')\geq$$
$$\geq \lceil \frac {k_0}{m}\rceil +\lfloor \frac {k'}{m}\rfloor +1= k+1\ .$$
This implies that $k'=0$ , $mk=k_0$ and $codim _{A_0} \Sigma _{k} ( \Delta _0)=k$. 
The corollary now follows directly from the first of the  
two theorems due to Ein and Lazarsfeld $\square$

